insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('sharing-a-bed-and-not-sleeping', 'Sharing a Bed and Not Sleeping', 'Snoring, different bedtimes, a duvet war and a phone screen at midnight. How to get your sleep back without it becoming a fight about the relationship.', 'You have been awake since two. They are asleep, warm, and faintly snoring. You have moved three times, considered the sofa, and are now lying there doing arithmetic about how much sleep you might still get.

By morning you are exhausted and slightly furious with someone who has done nothing wrong on purpose.

Why this gets so loaded so fast

Sleep deprivation makes everybody a worse version of themselves: shorter tempered, more easily hurt, less able to let things go.

So the conversation about the snoring happens between two people who are tired, and it rarely stays about the snoring. It becomes about consideration, or about who is more tired, or about whether you are being difficult.

And sharing a bed carries a lot of meaning. Any suggestion of changing it can sound like a verdict on the relationship rather than a practical fix, which is why people put up with years of bad sleep instead of saying anything.

Separate the problem from the person

Start by working out what is actually waking you, because the fixes are completely different.

Noise: snoring, teeth grinding, a phone buzzing, a partner who talks or shouts in their sleep.

Movement: restless legs, getting up for the bathroom, a mattress that transmits every turn.

Timing: different bedtimes, shift work, an alarm at five, someone who reads or scrolls in bed.

Temperature and bedding: one person too hot, one too cold, one duvet that cannot satisfy both.

Write down which of those it is and how often. That list is a practical problem to solve together, where "you keep waking me up" is an accusation.

Do the physical fixes first

A surprising amount of this is equipment rather than negotiation, and it is much easier to agree to.

Two separate duvets on one bed. This is the single most effective change most couples make and nobody regrets it.

Earplugs, properly fitted ones rather than the cheap foam, or a white noise machine or a fan. Blackout curtains. A bigger or less springy mattress if that is affordable.

Phones out of the bedroom, on a charger in the hall, with an actual alarm clock instead. A reading light that does not light the whole room.

And a quiet agreement about the hours: lights and screens off by a certain time, alarms set to vibrate, and getting dressed in another room when one of you is up first.

Take the snoring seriously rather than personally

Heavy snoring is a medical matter, not a character flaw, and it is worth pushing on.

If they snore loudly, gasp, stop breathing for moments, wake with headaches or a dry mouth, or fall asleep easily during the day, that may be sleep apnea, and it is both common and very treatable.

Say it as concern rather than complaint: I am worried about the way you stop breathing, will you get it looked at? Treating apnea improves their health and your nights at once.

Weight, alcohol in the evening, sleeping on the back, and a blocked nose all make snoring worse, and all are workable.

Sleeping apart is allowed

For some couples the honest answer is separate beds or separate rooms, some nights or most nights.

That is a practical arrangement, not a failure. Plenty of long, affectionate relationships run that way, and people are often astonished at how much better everything gets once they are both sleeping.

What matters is how it is framed and what you protect. Make it a joint decision rather than an exile, decide together who moves and when, and keep the things that mattered: going to bed together for a while, mornings, time in the same bed at weekends, physical closeness on purpose rather than by accident.

If one of you experiences it as rejection, say that out loud. The worry underneath is usually about closeness, and it can be answered directly instead of being argued through the sleeping arrangements.

Have the conversation in daylight

Never at two in the morning, and never immediately after a bad night.

Pick a neutral time, bring your list, and ask for something specific: can we try two duvets, can you see a doctor about the snoring, can the phone charge in the hall.

Offer something as well, because this is rarely one-sided; your alarm, your reading, your bathroom trip at four are probably on their list too.

And set a review date: let us try this for two weeks and see. A trial is much easier to agree to than a permanent change.

Protect your own sleep in the meantime

Keep your get-up time steady even after a broken night, because catching up by sleeping in pushes the next night later.

Get out of bed if you have been awake and frustrated for twenty minutes or so. Lying there fuming teaches your brain that bed is where you lie awake and resent somebody.

And do not keep count of the hours. The arithmetic is pure adrenaline and it never improves anything.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if either of you snores heavily or seems to stop breathing in the night, if you are exhausted during the day, or if broken sleep has gone on for weeks; both sleep apnea and insomnia have specific, effective treatments and neither needs to be endured.

A doctor can also point you toward the short course of therapy for insomnia that works better than sleeping tablets. And if the real problem is that the two of you cannot discuss anything without it turning into a row, a couples counselor is a reasonable thing to suggest; by then it is not about the duvet.', array['Sleep', 'Boundaries']::text[], 0, '2026-10-17T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('seeing-your-own-face-on-a-video-call', 'Seeing Your Own Face on a Video Call', 'A small window in the corner showing you, live, for an hour. Why video calls are harder than meetings in a room, and the settings and habits that take the edge off.', 'The call starts and there you are, bottom right, with the lighting from above and an expression you did not know you made. For the next fifty minutes you are both participating in a meeting and watching yourself do it.

By the end you have no clear memory of what was decided and a very clear memory of your own chin.

Why this is harder than a room

A video call asks you to do something nobody has ever had to do before: hold a conversation while continuously watching your own face.

In a room you cannot see yourself. On a call you can, in a small moving mirror, under unflattering light, from an angle nobody looks at you from in real life.

It also strips out the things that normally make conversation easy. Eye contact is impossible — you are either looking at them or at the camera. The tiny delays make you unsure whether to speak. Everyone is a motionless head, so you lose the ordinary signals that tell you how it is going.

Add a grid of other faces, any of which might be looking at you, and it is no wonder people find an hour of it more draining than a day in an office.

Turn your own picture off

Most platforms let you hide the view of yourself while everyone else still sees you. It is usually called hide self view.

Do that. It is the single biggest improvement available, it takes four seconds, and almost nobody knows it exists.

If you cannot find the setting, cover the corner of the screen with a sticky note. Low technology and completely effective.

And once your own face is gone, notice what happens to your attention: it goes back to the conversation, because self-monitoring was eating it.

Make the picture easier to live with

A few small changes, once, and then you never think about them again.

Put the light in front of you rather than above or behind: a window you face, or a lamp behind the screen. Overhead light is what produces the shadows people hate.

Raise the camera to eye level with a couple of books. A laptop on a desk shoots upward, which is the least flattering angle there is.

Sit back a little so your head is not filling the frame, and have something plain behind you.

None of that is vanity. It removes a real distraction, and most people find they feel noticeably more comfortable after ten minutes of setup.

Handle the attention, not the face

The uncomfortable feeling is largely self-focused attention: your spotlight is pointing inward, which makes every sensation and expression louder.

So point it outward on purpose. Listen to the actual words the other person is saying. Look at their face. Make a note by hand.

Keep your hands busy below the frame if that helps: a pen, a stress ball, your feet on the floor. And breathe out a little longer than you breathe in before you speak.

If you feel yourself heating up or reddening, let it happen rather than fighting it. The flush peaks and fades, and people on a screen notice it far less than you imagine.

Reduce the number of calls that need your face

It is worth saying plainly: not everything has to be on camera.

Cameras off for long internal meetings, audio only for one-to-ones where you are walking, a phone call instead of a video call when it would do. Many workplaces are happy with this and nobody has ever asked.

If a norm requires camera on, a short line usually settles it: I have mine off for the first part, I am listening. Or for a long day: I am going camera off between meetings to give my eyes a break.

And build in gaps. Back-to-back calls with no break is what turns ordinary discomfort into exhaustion.

Do not watch yourself back

Many platforms record, and the urge to watch your own contribution is strong.

Resist it, or watch it once with a specific purpose and then stop. Repeated reviewing is a form of checking: it narrows your attention onto what you dislike, makes it bigger, and produces no useful information.

The same goes for replaying the call in your head afterward. Write down any genuine action points, then do something else with your hands.

What people actually noticed

Almost nothing. They were looking at their own small window, worrying about their own lighting, and thinking about what to say next.

That is not reassurance; it is how attention works. Everyone on a video call is their own most watched participant.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if the dread of being on camera is shaping your work: turning down meetings, avoiding promotion, feeling sick before calls, or spending a long time afterward going over how you looked.

Social anxiety responds very well to treatment, usually in a small number of sessions, and the approach is close to what is above: dropping the self-monitoring, doing the calls, and letting the discomfort fade through repetition rather than avoidance.

And if how you feel about your appearance is keeping you out of ordinary life, or you are spending a long time every day checking or comparing, mention that to a doctor too. Those feelings are common and treatable, and nobody working with them will find them trivial.', array['Social Anxiety', 'Shame']::text[], 0, '2026-10-17T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-you-think-it-was-your-fault', 'When You Think It Was Your Fault', 'I should have left sooner. I should have known. Why self-blame is so common after something frightening happens, and how to look at it with the facts you actually had.', 'You have run it back a hundred times. The moment you could have said something. The lift you accepted. The warning you did not act on. The thing you did not notice about someone you trusted.

And every time, the verdict comes back the same: you should have known, and you did not, and therefore it was partly your fault.

Why blame lands on yourself

It is nearly universal after something frightening, and there are reasons that have nothing to do with what actually happened.

Blame feels better than helplessness. If it was something you did, then you have some control, and next time you could prevent it. The alternative — that bad things happen to people who did everything reasonably — is harder to live with.

You also know your own thoughts and nobody else''s. You remember your hesitation, your doubt, the moment you nearly said something, in detail. You cannot see the other person''s intent with anything like the same clarity, so your own part looks larger than it was.

And if you were ever blamed as a child for things outside your control, self-blame is a well-worn route. It arrives automatically rather than as a conclusion.

Hindsight is not evidence

The crucial distinction, and the one that does most of the work: what you know now is not what you had then.

Once you know the ending, every earlier moment looks like a signpost. The slightly odd message, the friend''s comment, the feeling in the car park — all obvious, afterward. Before, they were ordinary details among hundreds of others, most of which meant nothing.

So the useful question is not what could I have done. It is: with only what I knew at the time, and feeling what I was feeling, what was a reasonable thing to do?

Write that down properly. Not the film version with all the information, but the actual moment, with the actual fog in it.

What a frightened body does

People often blame themselves hardest for how they reacted rather than for what happened.

I froze. I did not fight. I went along with it. I was polite. I did not run.

Those are not choices. Under real threat the body does whatever it calculates will get you through: freeze, appease, go quiet, comply, go somewhere else in your head. That happens faster than thought and it is not available for reconsideration at the time.

Surviving something in the way your body chose is not consent and it is not cooperation. It is what the alarm system does.

Separate regret from responsibility

Both can be true at once, and conflating them is what keeps people stuck.

Regret: I wish I had not got in the car. I wish I had told someone earlier. That is real and it is allowed, and it does not assign fault.

Responsibility: who actually did the thing. If another person chose to hurt, deceive or endanger you, the responsibility is theirs, entirely, regardless of what you were wearing, drinking, trusting or hoping.

And where an accident is nobody''s fault at all, the honest answer is that it happened, and no amount of reassigning it will make it make sense.

Check the standard you are using

Ask whether you would hold anyone else to it.

If a friend told you exactly this story, with the same information available at the time, what would you say about their part in it? People in this position are almost always applying a standard to themselves that they would call absurd if it were applied to anybody else.

And notice the particular shapes self-blame takes. If only, which rewrites history. I should have known, which assumes information you did not have. I always, which turns one event into a character. Those are patterns rather than facts, and naming them takes some of their authority away.

Say it out loud to one person

Self-blame survives best in silence. It grows in the retelling you only do in your own head.

So tell one person who will not flinch: a friend, a counselor, a helpline, a group of people who have been through something similar. What most people find is not that they are talked out of it, but that saying it to another human loosens its grip.

Expect it to come back. This is rarely settled once; it is settled repeatedly, a bit more each time, usually over months.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the blame will not shift, if the memories are intruding during the day, if your sleep has broken down, or if you are avoiding places, people or ordinary things to keep the thoughts away.

Trauma treatment is specific and it works, including years afterward, and working on self-blame is one of the standard parts of it rather than something you have to get past first. Say plainly to whoever you see that you hold yourself responsible; it is a common thing to bring and it tells them exactly where to start.

And if it has taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Trauma', 'Thinking Traps']::text[], 0, '2026-10-17T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('saying-you-are-angry-before-you-are-furious', 'Saying You Are Angry Before You Are Furious', 'You say nothing at two out of ten, nothing at five, and then everything at nine. How to raise an annoyance while it is still small enough to be heard.', 'They left the dishes again. You said nothing. They were late again. You said nothing, slightly louder in your head. Then on Thursday they made a joke about the shopping and you heard yourself say something about the last four years.

They look genuinely startled, because from where they are standing this came out of nowhere. And now the conversation is about your reaction rather than about the dishes.

The cost of waiting for nine

Most people have one speed for anger: silence, then eruption. The middle is empty.

Waiting has real costs. Nobody can act on something they do not know about, so the thing keeps happening. The resentment compounds, which means by the time you speak, the subject is no longer the dishes; it is everything.

And at nine out of ten you are not able to be clear. You generalize, you bring up history, you say the thing you will regret. Which gives the other person an easy exit: they get to be upset about how you spoke instead of looking at what you raised.

Speaking at three out of ten is uncomfortable and far more effective.

Why the middle feels impossible

Usually because of what you learned about anger.

If anger in your house meant shouting, slammed doors or somebody frightening, then any level of it feels dangerous, and silence feels like the responsible choice.

If you were the easy one, the peacemaker, the child who did not add to anybody''s load, then mentioning an annoyance feels like being difficult.

And many people genuinely believe small things are not worth raising. The irony is that small things raised small stay small; small things swallowed become large.

What a three out of ten sounds like

Short, specific, about the thing rather than the person, and without a verdict attached.

Hey, the dishes were left again last night, can you do them before bed? Not: you never lift a finger.

I need to tell you something small before it gets big: when you are late and do not message, I spend the evening annoyed.

I did not like that joke. Said once, plainly, and then on with the evening.

Notice how little drama there is in those. That is the point. A small complaint said in a normal voice is not a confrontation; it is maintenance.

Say it close to the event

The longer the gap, the harder it is to raise and the less likely it is to land.

Same day, or next morning if the evening is not the moment. Not three weeks later, when they genuinely do not remember and you have rehearsed the case for the prosecution.

If you are too hot to speak well, take twenty minutes and come back. Too hot means raised heart rate, a speech forming, the urge to say something cutting. Twenty minutes of walking changes what you are capable of saying.

And pick the time deliberately: not mid-task, not in front of other people, not in the car on the way somewhere.

Name the level

This is a small trick that works unreasonably well: tell them how big it is.

This is a two out of ten, I just want to flag it. Or: this one actually matters to me, it is a seven.

People calibrate badly otherwise. They assume anything raised at all is serious, or that nothing is until you are shouting. Giving the number lets them respond proportionately, and it stops a two becoming an argument.

Expect it to be awkward at first

If you have been silent for years, the first few attempts will feel enormous and come out stiff. That is normal.

Start with the low-stakes ones: the wrong coffee, the radio volume, the meeting that always overruns. Practicing on small things builds the skill you will need for the big one.

And accept a mixed response. Some people will say thank you for telling me. Some will be defensive the first time and fine the second. What matters is that you said it at three rather than storing it for nine.

What to do with the anger you cannot raise

Some of it genuinely cannot be said: a boss who would punish it, a person who twists everything, someone who has died.

That still needs somewhere to go. Write it, walk it off, tell a friend, say it out loud in the car. Anger with no outlet does not evaporate; it leaks into the people who happen to be nearby.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if your anger is frightening you or anyone else, if you have broken things or made contact with someone in anger, if you are drinking when you are angry, or if the swing from silence to fury is a pattern you have tried and failed to change.

If speaking up at all feels impossible, that is equally worth taking to a counselor; it usually has a history, and it is one of the most workable things in therapy. Both ends of this — too much and none at all — respond well to help, and asking for it is not an admission of anything except that you would like the next ten years to go differently.', array['Anger', 'Assertiveness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-17T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('letting-a-good-moment-register', 'Letting a Good Moment Register', 'The nice things pass in seconds and the worries stay all day. A small, practical habit for letting good moments actually land, and why it is not positive thinking.', 'Something good happened today. The coffee was excellent, someone was kind at the till, the sky did something ridiculous on the way home.

Each of those got about two seconds before your attention moved to the email, the appointment, the thing you did not do. By this evening you will remember none of them, and you will remember the email clearly.

Why the good bits slide off

Your attention is not built for enjoyment. It is built to catch problems, and problems get priority.

Anything unresolved, risky or unfinished keeps your attention; anything pleasant and complete is released immediately, because there is nothing to do about it. So a kind remark gets two seconds and an awkward one gets the whole commute.

A low mood or a stretch of stress makes that gap wider. The pleasant stuff still arrives; it just does not stick to anything.

That is not a flaw in you and it is not something to be fixed by trying to feel grateful. It is about where attention goes and how long it stays, and that part is trainable.

The whole habit, in one line

When something small is good, stay with it for about twenty or thirty seconds on purpose.

That is it. Not a journal, not an exercise, not anything anyone can see. You simply notice, and then deliberately keep your attention on it a little longer than it would stay by itself.

The warmth of the cup. The dog''s weight against your leg. The silence after the house empties. The particular relief of getting into bed with clean sheets.

A few seconds of staying is what turns a passing event into something your day actually contains.

Make it physical rather than conceptual

The reason this works better than listing things you are grateful for is that the body is involved.

So notice where you feel it: the warmth in your chest, the loosening in your shoulders, the smile you did not plan. Attention on sensation holds much better than attention on an idea.

And use the senses: what you can see, the temperature, the sound. This is the same mechanism as grounding, pointed at something good rather than at something frightening.

If the feeling is small, let it be small. Nobody needs to be overcome by a cup of tea.

Pick a few reliable candidates

Most people have a handful of ordinary moments that are genuinely pleasant and completely unnoticed.

The first mouthful of a hot drink. Getting into the shower. The three steps outside in the morning air. Taking your shoes off. Putting the heating on. The song that is already in the car. A text from someone you like. Finishing something.

Choose two or three and let them be your practice rather than hunting for new ones. The point is repetition, not variety.

Expect it to feel pointless at first

For a few weeks this will seem too small to be doing anything, and some days the good moment will not register no matter how long you stay with it.

That is normal, particularly if your mood is low, and it is not evidence that it does not work. The change people notice first is not feeling happier; it is noticing more, and finding at the end of a day that there was something in it besides the problems.

And it is not an instruction to be positive. Nothing here asks you to reframe a bad day or be glad about something hard. The difficult things remain exactly as difficult; you are simply not letting the good ones go by unbanked.

When it is hard to stay with something good

A few people find that pausing on something pleasant makes them uneasy rather than warm.

That is worth knowing rather than pushing through. Sometimes it is a superstition about enjoying things — if I notice this, something will take it away. Sometimes it is grief: the good moment brings the person who is not here. And for some people, slowing down at all lets something underneath come up.

In those cases, keep it very short, three or four seconds, with your eyes open and your feet on the floor, and stop when you want to. There is no prize for staying longer.

Say it out loud sometimes

Telling somebody doubles the effect, mostly because you have to turn it into a sentence.

That was a good cup of coffee. I am glad we did that. Look at the sky. The dog is absurd.

This also happens to make you better company, which is a side effect nobody mentions.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if nothing at all has felt good for more than a couple of weeks, if things you used to enjoy have gone flat, or if that has come with changes in your sleep, appetite or energy.

Losing the ability to feel pleasure is one of the most recognizable signs that something is worth treating, and it is not laziness or a bad attitude; it lifts with treatment, and a doctor or a therapist is the right place to take it rather than trying harder at noticing. And if the hard things in your life are heavy enough that this kind of small practice feels like nothing against them, a counselor is a reasonable next step, because it is not meant to carry that weight on its own.', array['Mindfulness', 'Grounding']::text[], 0, '2026-10-17T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
